**user**:
> In C, how can I get the closest integer, always rounding *down* (and not just always rounding "toward zero"). So, -3.5 should give me -4.
**assistant**:
> Use `floor`, not a cast.
> 
> In C, a cast to `int` truncates toward zero:
> 
> ```c
> (int)  3.5   //  3
> (int) -3.5   // -3   (not what you want)
> ```
> 
> To always round *down* mathematically (toward negative infinity), use `floor` from `<math.h>`:
> 
> ```c
> #include <math.h>
> 
> double x = -3.5;
> int n = (int)floor(x);   // n == -4
> ```
> 
> Notes:
> 
> - `floor` returns a `double`, so you cast it to `int` afterward.
> - This works for both positive and negative values:
>   - `floor( 3.5)` → `3`
>   - `floor(-3.5)` → `-4`
> 
> If you’re starting from a `float`, you can still use `floor` (it will be promoted), or use `floorf` for clarity:
> 
> ```c
> float xf = -3.5f;
> int n = (int)floorf(xf);  // n == -4
> ```
